PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C. None of the above is correct.

The data show the ADC is substantially more potent than anti-TNF at reducing inflammation (ear swelling and paw swelling) at the 10 mg/kg dose used in Experiments 1 and 2. Bone-density loss (a proxy for osteoporosis risk) is also markedly lower with the ADC than with anti-TNF at the same dose. GRM itself reduces swelling and produces modest bone loss, so statements claiming the ADC is less effective than anti-TNF, that the two carry equal osteoporosis risk, or that GRM cannot fight inflammation are all contradicted by the results. Statements about “dosage chosen correctly” are likewise unsupported because GRM was deliberately given at only 3 mg/kg in Experiment 3. No listed option is factually consistent with the three experiments.}

}

